Assistant: Let's try a different approach by simplifying the component further and wrapping the imported component in an error boundary. The "Failed to fetch dynamically imported module" error might be related to how we're handling the import.

<file path="apps/web/.temp/dessn-component-routes/ServerPage-3e4baa8e.tsx">
  <change>
    <description>Simplify the component and handle dynamic import differently</description>
    <content>
import React, { Suspense } from 'react';
import { useParentState } from '../useIframeState';
import dynamic from 'next/dynamic';

// Dynamically import the component with no SSR
const DynamicComponent = dynamic(
  () => import('../../app/(use-page-wrapper)/apps/routing-forms/[...pages]/page'),
  { 
    ssr: false,
    loading: () => <div>Loading...</div>
  }
);

// Simple error boundary component
class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  state = { hasError: false, error: null };

  static getDerivedStateFromError(error) {
    return { hasError: true, error };
  }

  componentDidCatch(error, errorInfo) {
    console.error('Component Error:', error, errorInfo);
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.hasError) {
      return <div>Error loading component. Please try again.</div>;
    }
    return this.props.children;
  }
}

export default function ComponentPreview() {
  const [state] = useParentState({
    params: {
      type: "string",
      value: JSON.stringify({ pages: ["routing-link", "form1"] }),
      label: "Params",
    },
    searchParams: {
      type: "string",
      value: JSON.stringify({ query: "example" }),
      label: "Search Params",
    },
  });

  let params;
  let searchParams;
  
  try {
    params = JSON.parse(state.params.value);
    searchParams = JSON.parse(state.searchParams.value);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error parsing params:', error);
    params = { pages: ["routing-link", "form1"] };
    searchParams = { query: "example" };
  }

  return (
    <ErrorBoundary>
      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
        <DynamicComponent params={params} searchParams={searchParams} />
      </Suspense>
    </ErrorBoundary>
  );
}
</content>
  </change>
</file>

Key changes made:

1. Removed all providers temporarily to isolate the import issue
2. Used Next.js's `dynamic` import instead of direct import
3. Disabled SSR for the dynamic component
4. Enhanced error boundary with better error handling
5. Simplified the overall component structure
6. Kept the core functionality of parameter handling
7. Maintained Suspense for loading states

The main focus is on:
1. Properly handling the dynamic import
2. Providing better error boundaries
3. Reducing complexity that might interfere with module loading
4. Maintaining essential functionality

This simplified version should help identify if the issue is with the providers or with the module import itself. If this works, we can gradually add back the providers one by one to identify which one might be causing issues.

